ModelMesh: write one PROTO chunk per attachment, as the reader reads them.

`ModelMesh::save` packed every attachment into a single `PROTO` chunk, while
`ModelMesh::load` takes exactly one attachment from each `PROTO` chunk it sees:

    case ModelMeshChunkType::PROTO:
        this->attachments[attachment_names[this->attachments.size()]].load_from_section(c);
        break;

So a model saved with N attachments read back with one, and the other N-1 were
lost silently — no error, just a prop missing everything except its first part.

The reader is the side that matches the format. Measured against a Gothic II
install through the chunk the reader produces, retail models come back with
several attachments each — 2 for `CHESTSMALL_NW_POOR_OPEN` (base and lid), 4
for `CHESTSMALL_NW_POOR_LOCKED` (base, lid, lock and its zone mesh) and 4 for
`RMAKER_1` (three rings and the maker) — which is only possible if retail
writes one `PROTO` chunk per attachment. 63 of NewWorld's 63 MODEL visuals read
this way; the histogram over them is 7 models with no attachments, 37 with one,
10 with two and 9 with four.

The `NODES` chunk already writes the names in `attachments` iteration order and
the reader pairs the i-th chunk with the i-th name, so emitting one chunk per
attachment in that same order is all that is needed.

